nikgobd: the music.nikgob player without a window (Raspberry Pi, server)

Files
  nikgobd           the binary
  nikgobd.toml      example config (also: nikgobd --print-config)
  nikgobd.service   systemd user unit

Install (as the user who owns the sound, not root)
  install -Dm755 nikgobd ~/.local/bin/nikgobd
  install -Dm644 nikgobd.toml ~/.config/nikgob/nikgobd.toml
  install -Dm644 nikgobd.service ~/.config/systemd/user/nikgobd.service
  # edit ~/.config/nikgob/nikgobd.toml: library_dir (this machine's own
  # copy of the music, never a folder the desktop app uses), output_device
  sudo loginctl enable-linger "$USER"
  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now nikgobd

Linger keeps the user's services, PulseAudio among them, running without
a login session. Without it the player and PulseAudio stop when the last
session ends.

Output
  pactl list short sinks
The second column is the sink name to put in output_device. A sink that is
not there plays on the default output until it comes back.

Logs and state
  journalctl --user -u nikgobd -f
  systemctl --user status nikgobd
  ~/.local/share/nikgobd/crash.log      panics
  ~/.local/share/nikgobd/               library database, prefs, session

Trying it by hand (stop the service first: one nikgobd per data dir)
  nikgobd --console
then type help; e.g. "liked", "duck 0.18 120 8000", "unduck", "status",
"device alsa_output.usb-3142_fifine_Headset-00.analog-stereo".

Stop and remove
  systemctl --user disable --now nikgobd
  rm ~/.local/bin/nikgobd ~/.config/systemd/user/nikgobd.service

Runtime packages: libasound2 (Debian/Raspberry Pi OS) or alsa-lib (Arch),
and a PulseAudio server (or PipeWire's pulse service) for the user. One
nikgobd per data dir: a second one on the same dir exits at once.
